  "_doc": "Where the colony's vehicles park and the roads they drive (round 15, task GATE; written by tools/make_motor_pool.py, checked by tools/check_motor_pool.py: change the choices there and run it again, do not edit the points by hand). Tiles, x east, y south; a heading is in degrees, 0 east, 90 south, 270 north. THE PLACE: the motor pool is the apron inside the main gate (`apron` [x, y, w, h]); the gate is the opening in the north wall (wall segment 2): its middle is `gate.x`, the free opening between the two gate posts is `gate.free` wide (the leaves slide out of it behind the neighbouring wall pieces), and a car passing it is under the walkway's bridge between `gate.y_north` and `gate.y_south`; the street's eastbound lane (the south side) has its middle at `lane_y`, and the route's ends are `edge_west` (where a car comes onto the map) and `edge_east` (where it leaves). `zones` are the rectangles [x0, y0, x1, y1] a car's body (2.2 wide, 4.4 long) may be in: lane, junction (where it turns between the lane and the gate), gate (between the pallets and the bus), posts (between the two gate posts), bridge (under the catwalk bridge, between the ends of the deck's lattice) and apron. THE SPOTS: `at` is the middle of the parked car, nose to the north; a car leaves FORWARD (`out_route`: bay, aisle, gate, a right turn into the lane, east) and arrives FORWARD through the gate (`in_route`: west edge, lane, a right turn into the gate, down to the top of the apron, a swing round (forward, one way round the aisle's middle, then a few tiles in reverse the other way) to face north, then a REVERSING stretch down the aisle and into the bay). A route is a list of points {x, y, r, rev, h}: x and y the middle of the car; r the corner radius (0 everywhere: the corners are already cut into points); rev true on a point means the leg that ENDS at that point is driven in reverse (the nose keeps its heading while the car moves backwards); h the nose's heading, given on every point of the apron, the swing and the reverse: interpolate it between the points (unwrap the angle). Where `h` is missing the nose is the direction of the leg (turned round on a reversing leg). THE BUS: a vehicle longer than a bay (the bus, 2.6 x 8.07) takes the spot with `long: true` (the aisle end, id 2), whose `at` is the BUS's middle (so that its 8.07 tail is flush with the apron's end: it stands in the aisle between the two side bays, 2.6 wide in a 3.1 aisle); it swings through the gate and the apron on the same route and its ends overhang the apron at the swing (tools/check_motor_pool.py sweeps a sedan-sized body only; it does check the bus parked). THREE SPOTS: a fourth bay would stand behind another and could not leave forward, so the pool holds three and a fourth vehicle has to share a place; the aisle is 3.1 wide (between the cars in the two side bays), the gate's free opening 4.84.",
